.TH CHTTPSVR_SET_ENGINE_LOGGER 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_set_engine_logger \- set the logger for engine-level diagnostics
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_set_engine_logger(clog cl);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Derives a child logger from cl, adds the field component=http-engine,
and installs it as the shared reactor's diagnostics logger.  Any
previously installed engine logger is closed.

The reactor logs to this handle at exactly three points: a TLS
handshake failure, a listen-socket bind failure, and each idle-timeout
sweep pass that closes one or more connections.  If no engine logger
has been installed when the engine first starts, a fallback logger
(fd 2, level CLOG_FATAL) is installed automatically, mirroring
create_chttpsvr's own internal stderr/FATAL-only logger; since all
three diagnostic points above log below CLOG_FATAL, that fallback
logger's min_level filters every one of them out, so it is silent in
practice unless this function is used to install a more verbose one.

May be called at any time, including before the first chttpsvr_start,
while the reactor is already running, or after a full engine stop; the
reactor's own diagnostics logger is scoped to the reactor's lifetime
(see chttpsvr_engine_stop), so a full stop/restart cycle requires
calling this again if engine-level logging is still wanted afterward.

cl must not be NULL; the derived child logger is owned by the engine
and must not be closed by the caller.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args if cl is NULL.
ccol_not_enough_memory if the child logger cannot be allocated.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_wait (3)
